CALL 368  id gnews:9f430c6ce2e8d551fe1f063a, category T7, author mlq.ai (media), published 2026-07-07, title: DeepSeek Designing Proprietary AI Inference Chip to Cut Nvidia, Huawei Dependence

DeepSeek is developing a custom chip designed for AI inference — generating model responses — rather than training, Reuters reported citing three sources [1]
The project has been underway for about a year, with DeepSeek quietly recruiting chip engineers and holding talks with foundries and memory suppliers [2]
Nvidia shares fell roughly 1.2% on the day to $193.17, though the stock remains up 22% year-over-year [3]
DeepSeek recently closed a record $7.5 billion funding round at a valuation above $50 billion, its first outside capital raise [4]
U.S. export controls bar Chinese designers from accessing the most advanced overseas foundries and high-bandwidth memory, posing a major manufacturing hurdle [2]
DeepSeek, the Hangzhou-based AI startup behind some of China's most advanced large language models, is developing its own custom AI chip, Reuters reported on Monday, citing three people with knowledge of the matter [1] . The chip is designed for inference — the phase where trained models generate responses to user queries — rather than for training new models.
The project has been underway for roughly a year and remains in early stages, with DeepSeek quietly expanding its chip engineering team without public job postings [2] . The company is in discussions with chip design firms, semiconductor foundries, and memory suppliers, though no specific partners have been named.
The move marks a significant strategic expansion for DeepSeek, which burst onto the global AI stage in January 2025 with models that rivaled U.S. frontrunners at a fraction of the compute cost. It comes weeks after the company closed a record $7.5 billion funding round — its first outside capital — at a valuation exceeding $50 billion, with backing from Tencent, CATL, and founder Liang Wenfeng himself [4] .
DeepSeek's chip targets the fastest-growing segment of AI computing. Inference workloads — running trained models for end users — now account for the majority of AI chip demand as companies deploy models at scale. Custom inference chips are typically cheaper and more power-efficient than the general-purpose GPUs sold by Nvidia, making them attractive for companies seeking to compress costs [2] .
The strategy aligns with DeepSeek's broader cost-efficiency playbook. In May 2026, the company cut prices on its V4-Pro API by 75%, intensifying a price war among Chinese AI providers [5] . Controlling its own silicon would give DeepSeek further leverage to undercut rivals on inference pricing.
Designing a competitive AI chip typically takes years and billions of dollars in investment. For a Chinese company, the challenge is compounded by U.S. export controls that bar access to the most advanced overseas foundries and restrict shipments of high-bandwidth memory — a component critical to inference chip performance [2] .
Analyst Richard Windey highlighted the constraint: the same export rules that have effectively zeroed out Nvidia's China revenue also limit DeepSeek's ability to manufacture or sell advanced silicon outside China [3] .
Chinese companies are nevertheless accelerating domestic chip adoption. Industry surveys indicate Chinese firms plan to shift 46% of their AI-accelerator budgets to domestic suppliers within 12 months, up from roughly 30% today [5] . DeepSeek's chip effort fits squarely within that trend.
DeepSeek is not the only AI lab pursuing proprietary silicon. OpenAI and Anthropic have both disclosed custom chip programs, while Google has long run its TPU chips for both training and inference. Amazon designs its Trainium and Inferentia chips for AWS customers [2] .
Within China, Huawei's Ascend line of AI accelerators has emerged as the primary domestic alternative to Nvidia. DeepSeek's in-house effort positions the startup as a competitor to Huawei in inference hardware — not just in AI models [5] .
No timeline for tape-out or production has been disclosed. The project's early-stage status means a finished chip is likely years away, and manufacturing constraints under current U.S. export rules represent a structural bottleneck [2] .
DeepSeek's $7.5 billion war chest gives it the capital to sustain a multi-year hardware development effort. Founder Liang Wenfeng personally contributed roughly $2.9 billion to the round, and the deal's unusual structure — a limited partnership with a five-year lock-up for most investors — suggests the company is planning on a long horizon [4] .


